artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $\mathcal{S}$ denote the smallest class of (Hausdorff) topological groups satisfying all of the following:
(i) $\mathcal{S}$ contains every compact separable topological group;
(ii) $\mathcal{S}$ contains every countable topological group;
(iii) $\mathcal{S}$ is closed under finite direct products;
(iv) $\mathcal{S}$ is closed under taking closed subgroups;
(v) $\mathcal{S}$ is closed under taking continuous homomorphic images (i.e., if $G \in \mathcal{S}$ and $\varphi\colon G \to H$ is a continuous surjective homomorphism onto a topological group $H$, then $H \in \mathcal{S}$).

Determine whether the following characterization holds: a topological group $G$ belongs to $\mathcal{S}$ if and only if there exists a compact separable subgroup $K \le G$ such that the coset space $G/K$ is countable.

